# Chapter Eleven — The Fifth Cover

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.3. Cut by a third after the pass-2 review: the file opens at the fifth pocket, and Ruth's call to Ward is off the page except for six o'clock. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 31 — Ruth's office

= Present: Henry and Ruth; Nell arrives later.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth has offered one file, not her office. Henry must respect the line she draws, and what is on her worktable is on her side of it.

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE, WEST THIRD - AFTERNOON

Ruth's office is on the second floor above a printer on West Third. The presses make the stair rail tremble until twelve, when the printer closes for lunch.

At twelve seventeen Friday afternoon, the building is still.

Black letters on Ruth's frosted-glass door read:

!RUTH ELLERY

!COPYWRITING

Henry looks at the glass.

RUTH
Say it.

HENRY
Your name is larger than mine.

RUTH
I ordered the door.

She unlocks it.

There is one outer room, one inner office, and no receptionist. The outer room holds a typewriter table, a long worktable, and four product photographs with white paper laid over the words beneath them. A bottle of floor wax shines in a kitchen nobody has used. A smiling man points toward a blank rectangle where the price should be.

Henry stops beside the worktable.

HENRY
Where is Nell?

RUTH
Havel asked her to approve the wording of the hold notice. She said half an hour.

Ruth hangs her handbag behind the inner-office door and folds her jacket over the back of her chair. Her desk faces a wall of shallow filing drawers. None of them is locked.

HENRY
You were here yesterday morning.

RUTH
Before I brought you Paul's address. I looked through the desk, found his key, and called Ward.

HENRY
Did anyone come with you?

RUTH
No.

HENRY
Did you remove anything?

RUTH
The envelope with the address and key. Both went to Ward.

HENRY
Anything else?

RUTH
No.

She meets his eyes before opening the middle drawer.

RUTH
You may look at the Ellery file. I turn the pages. If something else is open on my desk, that does not make it yours.

HENRY
Agreed.

She takes out a narrow black expanding file. A piece of masking tape on its flap reads P.E. in Ruth's hand. Beneath that, written later, is 418-R.

Ruth sets it between them but keeps one hand on the flap.

RUTH
At the bungalow, did you believe me?

HENRY
About which part?

RUTH
The pages.

HENRY
They were yours.

RUTH
That was not the part I asked you to believe.

HENRY
I believe you did not know it was June Avery.

Ruth removes her hand from the flap.

RUTH
You left yourself room.

HENRY
So did you.

Ruth opens the file.

## Scene 32 — The file

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Six paper pockets divide the file, MAY to SEPTEMBER. Each holds a stack of onion-skin carbons and a small account card. The carbons are headed only DIALOGUE PASS, without title, character, studio, or address. Every alteration is initialed. When Paul returned correspondence, he initialed the card's received line.

RUTH
Six assignments. My carbons and my correspondence. The source pages went back with the originals.

HENRY
Why keep the carbons?

RUTH
Because I wrote them.

She says it without defense.

The outer door opens. Nell enters carrying the gray hold notice from Bungalow Six.

NELL
Am I late?

RUTH
Eleven minutes.

Nell lays the notice facedown on the worktable, over the white paper, and does not look at what is under it.

NELL
Havel kept changing *permission* to *clearance*.

RUTH
Which did she use?

NELL
Permission.

There are two chairs in the inner office. Ruth gives hers to Nell and brings the low typewriter chair in from outside. Henry remains standing beside the file.

RUTH
You may read everything in this file. I turn the pages.

Nell reads the fourth pass without speaking. Ruth turns each page after Nell nods. The inherited sentences occupy little space. A gesture survives in one margin; a reply survives in another. Around them, Ruth has removed explanations, shortened entrances, and made interruptions arrive earlier. Nell does not try to separate each sheet into what belonged to June and what belongs to Ruth.

NELL
You marked everything you changed.

RUTH
I was protecting my work.

NELL
From whom?

RUTH
I thought from Paul.

Nell nods once. Ruth turns to the fifth pocket.

## Scene 33 — The fifth cover

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

The dialogue carbons are present. Behind them is a carbon copy of the cover Ruth sent back to Paul with the finished pages. Three ordinary notes appear at the top: a repeated page number, a missing entrance, and one line that could not be heard clearly.

At the bottom, separated from the rest, Ruth typed:

PRIVATE RECORDING?

The pressure of the question has cut a faint second line into the carbon.

Ruth removes the account card from the same pocket. In the space for material returned, she wrote:

!P.E.: REHEARSAL. FAMILY CLEARED.

Below the note, RECEIVED is initialed P.E. in Paul's hand.

NELL
You said at the bungalow that you asked him.

RUTH
I asked him on paper. He answered on paper. I wrote down what he answered so I would remember that I had asked.

NELL
And the sixth pass is dated nine days later.

RUTH
Nine days. I have counted them since.

Nell puts the fifth card back exactly where Ruth removed it.

HENRY
Where is the cover Paul answered?

RUTH
He wrote on the original and returned it with the sixth assignment. It should be here.

She lifts the fifth pocket by its sides. It contains no other sheet. She checks behind the sixth pocket and closes the file halfway.

RUTH
No. Paul took it.

HENRY
When?

RUTH
Wednesday afternoon. At four ten. He came here.

HENRY
Ward knows you saw him?

RUTH
Yes. He asked why Paul came. I said he collected old work correspondence. Until this morning I did not understand why that page mattered.

Ruth opens a loose-leaf desk diary. Wednesday's square holds one entry in her hand:

!4:10 — P.E. / COVER 5

RUTH
He asked for the cover with my question and his answer. I gave it to him. He said somebody had finally asked him to put his answers beside the questions.

NELL
Who?

RUTH
He would not say.

HENRY
What did you ask him?

RUTH
Whether he was in trouble. He said, "Not if I get there tomorrow."

HENRY
Havel.

RUTH
I know that now.

NELL
And you gave him the page proving he had lied to you.

RUTH
The page proved I asked once.

NELL
It proved what he answered.

RUTH
That too.

Henry does not write. His notebook remains in his coat.

HENRY
What was Paul like when he came here?

RUTH
Late.

HENRY
He was on time.

RUTH
For the appointment. Late for everything else.

She looks at the empty fifth pocket.

RUTH
He stood where you are. He asked if I still had the page where he answered me. I said, "You mean the page where you didn't." He said, "That one."

NELL
Was he frightened?

RUTH
No. He was pleased with himself.

NELL
For telling the truth?

RUTH
For having decided to.

Nell lets go of the account card.

RUTH
I wanted to dislike him for that.

NELL
Did you?

RUTH
Yes.

NELL
Only for that?

Ruth does not answer.

Henry points to the sixth pocket. Ruth opens it. Her final carbons and their cover carbon remain. The original cover is absent; its card is marked OUT WITH ORIGINALS.

RUTH
Paul never returned the sixth cover. There was no seventh assignment to bring it back with.

HENRY
So the sixth is absent in the ordinary way. The fifth was here, and Paul came back for it.

RUTH
He came back for it.

HENRY
Did you see either cover in the carton downstairs?

Ruth closes the sixth pocket.

RUTH
No.

HENRY
You saw typed pages and envelopes.

RUTH
I did not read them.

HENRY
I asked whether you saw them.

RUTH
I did not see either cover.

Nell looks from Ruth to the black file.

NELL
You said at the bungalow that the work should carry your name for the record. I will not call your sentences my mother's. I will not let them call hers yours.

RUTH
All right.

NELL
That is not permission.

RUTH
I know.

Nell stands.

NELL
Call Ward.

## Scene 34 — Six o'clock

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Ruth moves the telephone from the corner of her desk. Before dialing, she switches off the ceiling fan. The loose sheets settle inside the open pockets.

She tells Ward, in a voice that does not carry past the desk, that she has opened her file, that Paul collected her fifth cover at four ten Wednesday, that it carried her question and his answer, and that the original is gone. She gives him permission to retain the whole black file. Ward speaks long enough that she sits down. Then she gives Henry the receiver.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Calder, I have the store after six. Bell, Price, and Ellery will walk their routes separately. You and Miss Avery may observe now that scene processing is closed, provided neither of you interferes.

HENRY
What changed?

WARD (TELEPHONE)
My people finished underneath the gallery.

HENRY
What did they find?

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Six o'clock.

Ruth leans toward the telephone.

RUTH
I already gave you my route.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Wednesday I took your account. Tonight you show me where you stood.

RUTH
I came out of the lift with Paul. Mr. Bell gave him the carton and went into receiving. Paul gave me the key. He opened the carton beside the display-access door. We argued. I left him there and returned upstairs.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
You did not open the access door.

RUTH
No.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Then you will show me where you turned back.

RUTH
Yes.

Ward asks for Henry again.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Do not bring your notebook into the gallery.

HENRY
Why?

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Because there is nowhere clean to put it.

The line goes dead.

Henry replaces the receiver.

The presses below start again. The floor trembles. The ceiling fan turns once without power and stops. The fifth pocket remains open.
